  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Your teacher will give your group a <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/56e713d00201166b295ef9eb86556dab08c90020">set of cards</link>. Each card contains a graph or an equation.</para>
            <para>Take turns with your partner to sort the cards into sets so that each set contains two equations and a graph that all represent the same quadratic function.</para>
            <para>For each set of cards that you put together, explain to your partner how you know they belong together.</para>
            <para>For each set that your partner puts together, listen carefully to their explanation. If you disagree, discuss your thinking and work to reach an agreement.</para>
            <para>Once all the cards are sorted and discussed, record the equivalent equations, sketch the corresponding graph, and write a brief note or explanation about why the representations were grouped together.</para>
            <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>Set 1: 
    Standard Form: \(y=x^2-1\) 
    Factored Form: \(y=(x+1)(x-1)\)</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_09_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
            <para>Set 2:
    Standard Form: \(y=x^2-4x\) 
    Factored Form: \(y=x(x-4)\)</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_09_02.svg"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
            <para>Set 3:
    Standard Form: \(y=x^2-5x+4\) 
    Factored Form: \(y=(x-1)(x-4)\)</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_09_03.svg"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
            <para>Set 4:
    Standard Form: \(y=x^2-4x+4\) 
    Factored Form: \(y=(x-2)^2\)</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_09_04.svg"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>

    <note class="self-check">
      <para>Examine the graph below.</para>
      <para>
          <media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UPWARD WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 2 AND \(x\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 2 AND 1.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_10_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para>Which function matches the graph?</para>
    </note>

    <section>
      <title>Matching Quadratic Functions and Their Graphs</title>
      <para>Below is a quadratic function represented in standard form, factored form, and with a graph.</para>
      <para>Standard Form: \(f(x)=x^2+2x-8\)
  Factored Form: \(f(x) = (x + 4)(x - 2)\)</para>
      <para>
          <media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UPWARD WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 8 AND \(x\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 4 AND 2.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_11_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para>Notice the factored form helps find the zeros: \(x = -4\) and \(x = 2\).</para>
      <para>The factored form gives the zeros since the zeros (\(x\)-intercepts) are located where each factor equals 0.</para>
      <para>The standard form, \(f(x)=x^2+2x-8\), helps give the \(y\)-intercept. The \(y\)-intercept occurs when \(x = 0\). Here, \(f(0) = -8\). On the graph, the \(y\)-intercept is \((0, -8)\).</para>
      <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Matching Quadratic Functions and Their Graphs</title>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Given the graph below and its function in standard form and factored form, identify all intercepts.</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UPWARD WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 6 AND \(x\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 2 AND 3.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_11_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
            <para>Standard form: \(f(x)=x^2-x-6\)
      Factored form: \(f(x) = (x - 3)(x + 2)\)</para>
            <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Here is how to find the intercepts with the information provided:</para>
            <para>The graph and the factored form help give the \(x\)-intercepts. They occur where the factors of the factored form equal 0, at \(x = -2\) and \(x = 3\). On the graph, these are where the graph crosses the \(x\)-axis, at the points \((-2, 0)\) and \((3, 0)\).</para>
            <para>The \(y\)-intercept can be found with standard form when \(x=0\) in \(f(x)=x^2-x-6\). So \(y = -6\). 
    Notice on the graph, the \(y\)-intercept, where the graph crosses the \(y\)-axis, is at \((0, -6)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
  </content>
